A declarative query says which answers count. A plan says how to obtain them. This separation allows a planner to use one index for a small input and another for a large one, but it works only if the language specifies what counts as the same answer. Are repeated results counted? Is the order observed? Is a result a pair of document identifiers, or a pair plus a witness path? These decisions change which plans are sound.

\section{Specify Logic and Search Separately}

Maarten van Emden and Robert Kowalski's \emph{The Semantics of Predicate Logic as a Programming Language} relates operational and fixed-point accounts of positive logic programs. The fixed point characterizes what the rules entail; a search procedure determines when derivations will actually be found. In \emph{Logic Programming in Scheme}, Nils M. Holm shows how unification makes logic variables useful in queries: matching a goal with a rule can bind variables in either expression. This is more general than a function call receiving only ground arguments. A searcher must also delimit the bindings to a branch so that a failed choice does not contaminate its alternatives.

Rule syntax does not determine search fairness. A left-to-right depth-first search, as in conventional Prolog, can follow a recursive candidate forever while a finite derivation is available through another candidate. Putting a base clause first can help particular queries but is not a completeness guarantee for an infinite search. For positive function-free rules over a finite database, a bottom-up evaluator can instead add derived facts until there are no more; its termination depends on finiteness, not on the luck of a clause ordering.

\section{Worked Example: Citation Reachability}

Index documents by stable identifier and record hyperlinks classified as citations. Ask which pairs of documents are connected by a nonempty citation chain. In a set-valued rule notation:

\begin{verbatim}
reach(X,Y) :- cites(X,Y).
reach(X,Y) :- cites(X,Z), reach(Z,Y).
\end{verbatim}

With extensional facts \texttt{cites(a,b)}, \texttt{cites(b,c)}, and \texttt{cites(c,b)}, the first round produces the three direct pairs. A second round adds \((a,c)\), \((b,b)\), and \((c,c)\). A third round adds nothing. A self-pair is returned because the rules mean a nonempty chain and there are cycles, not because the evaluator has inserted zero-length paths. Document \texttt{a} does not reach itself. A bag of all walks would not even be finite for this input, which is why a rewrite from endpoint sets to path enumeration is not neutral.

The running invariant is that each stored pair has a witness chain, and after the \(k\)-th round the set includes all pairs with a chain of length at most \(k\). Joining new pairs from the previous round with \texttt{cites} and discarding already known pairs is enough: extensions of older pairs have already been attempted. A regression test should check the exact six pairs, add an isolated document, and vary fact order and index selection. If edges may be deleted, growth-only delta evaluation is insufficient; one must retract unsupported results or recompute, since a pair may have several distinct witnesses.

\section{Choose What the Query Exposes}

Oskar van Rest and colleagues' \emph{PGQL: a Property Graph Query Language} uses graph patterns and regular path queries, with conditions on labels and properties. Reporting that a path of citation edges exists is weaker than returning a shortest path or all matching paths. A regular expression over edge labels can be checked by traversal over pairs of graph vertices and automaton states; for existential queries, revisiting an identical pair need not expand its successors again. Returning witness paths would require additional state or reconstruction rules.

The query depends on extraction decisions. Udo Kruschwitz's \emph{Intelligent Document Retrieval: Exploiting Markup Structure} studies building domain knowledge from markup: a link inside a bibliography can be interpreted differently from one in site navigation. Test those interpretations in the ingest layer, not by hiding the differences behind query rewrites. The \href{dossier-on-modern-cc-design/chapters/07-compiler-as-a-database.md}{dossier's Compiler as a Database chapter} puts similar pressure on the representation of facts: one must distinguish what was recorded from what may safely be recomputed.

