\section{Return One Shortest Label-Constrained Witness}

Suppose a document query asks not just whether a link pattern matches but for one shortest witness. Take a finite directed graph with unit-cost labeled edges, two document identifiers, and a deterministic finite automaton for the accepted sequence of labels. The query returns an ordered list of edge identifiers, or no match. To make ties reproducible, traverse outgoing edges in ascending stable identifier order. The important change from a vertex-only search is that path prefixes ending at the same document can still have different futures under the automaton.

\begin{verbatim}
witness(graph, machine, source, target):
  start = (source, machine.start)
  parent = {start: none}; push start into FIFO queue
  while queue is not empty:
    (v, q) = pop front of queue
    if v == target and accepting(q):
      # Reconstruct exact edges, including parallel ones.
      return reversed_parent_edges(parent, (v, q))
    for edge in outgoing(v) in identifier order:
      next = advance(q, edge.label)
      if next is undefined: continue
      state = (edge.target, next)
      # The automaton state is part of visited identity.
      if state in parent: continue
      parent[state] = ((v, q), edge.id)
      push state into queue
  return NoMatch
\end{verbatim}

Let the pattern accept one or more \texttt{cite} labels followed by a \texttt{supplement} label. From \(a\) there are two edges to \(b\): a \texttt{nav} edge numbered \(1\) and a \texttt{cite} edge numbered \(2\). There is a \texttt{cite} loop at \(b\) numbered \(3\) and a \texttt{supplement} edge from \(b\) to \(c\) numbered \(4\). The query from \(a\) to \(c\) returns \verb|[2, 4]|. Deleting edge \(4\) returns \texttt{NoMatch}, even though the loop offers infinitely many finite prefixes.

